{{smaller|'''The three R's.''' See ''R.''—'''The Three Sisters.''' See ''sister.''—'''Three-armed cross,''' a figure composed of three lines parting from a common center, either in the form of a Y (see ''Y-cross''), or composed of three hooks as if a figure in revolution, or of three arms broken at an angle, and bending all in the same direction. See ''triskele.''—'''Three-card monte.''' See ''monte.''—'''Three-cylinder steam-engine,''' a triple expansion-cylinder steam-engine. See ''steam-engine.''—'''Three-day fever,''' dengue.—'''Three-em brace,''' in ''printing,'' a brace three ems wide.—'''Three estates.''' See ''estate,'' 9.—'''Three-field system.''' See ''field.''—'''Three hours.''' See ''hour.''—'''Three kings of Cologne.''' See ''king''<sup>1</sup>.—'''Three-line letter,''' in ''printing,'' an initial letter which is the height of three lines of the body of the type of the text in which it is used.—'''Three-mile limit, zone,''' or '''belt.''' See ''mile.''—'''Three-million bill.''' See ''million''<sup>1</sup>.—'''Three sheets in the wind.''' See ''a sheet in the wind,'' under ''sheet''<sup>1</sup>.—'''Three thirds†, three threads†,''' a mixture of three malt liquors, formerly in demand, as equal parts of ale, beer, and twopenny. Compare ''entire'' and ''porter''<sup>3</sup>.}}

{{smaller block|Ezekiel Driver, of Puddle-dock, carman, having disorder'd his pia mater with too plentiful a morning's draught of ''three-threads'' and old Pharaoh, had the misfortune to have his cart run over him.
{{right|''Tom Brown,'' Works, II. 286. (''Davies.'')}}}}

{{smaller|'''Three times three,''' three cheers thrice repeated.}}

{{smaller block|Again the feast, the speech, the glee, . . .
The crowning cup, the ''three-times-three.''
{{right|''Tennyson,'' In Memoriam, Conclusion.}}}}

{{smaller block|Before I sit down I must give you a toast to be drunk with ''three-times-three'' and all the honours.
{{right|''T. Hughes,'' Tom Brown at Rugby, i. 6.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Three trees†,''' the gallows, formed by a transverse beam on two uprights.}}

{{smaller block|For commonly such knaues as these
Doe end their lyves vpon ''three trees.''
{{right|''Breton,'' Toyes of an Idle Head, p. 28. (''Davies.'')}}}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' 1. A number the sum of two and one.

{{smaller block|My face so thin
That in my ear I durst not stick a rose,
Lest men should say, "Look, where ''three-farthings'' goes!"
{{right|''Shak.,'' K. John, i. 1. 143.}}}}

{{smaller block|He values me at a crack'd ''three-farthings,'' for aught I see.
{{right|''B. Jonson,'' Every Man in his Humour, ii. 1.}}}}

'''threefold''' (thrē′fōld), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< ME. ''threfold, threovold, threfald,'' < AS. ''thrīfeald, thriefeald, thriefald, threófeald'' (= OFries. ''thrifald'' = MLG. ''drēvalt, drivolt'' = OHG. ''drifalt,'' MHG. ''drivalt'' = Icel. ''threfaldr;'' also, with added adj. termination, = D. ''drievoudig'' = OHG. ''drifalt,'' MHG. ''drivalt, drivaltec,'' G. ''dreifältig'' = Sw. ''trefaldig'' = Dan. ''trefoldig''), < ''threó,'' three, + ''-feald,'' E. ''-fold.''] '''I.''' ''a.'' Consisting of three in one, or one thrice repeated; multiplied by three; triple: as, ''threefold'' justice.

{{smaller block|A ''threefold'' cord is not quickly broken. Eccles. iv. 12.}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' The bog-bean, ''Menyanthes trifoliata.''

'''threefold''' (thrē′fōld), ''adv.'' In a threefold manner; trebly; thrice: often used in an intensive way, with the sense of 'much' or 'greatly.'

{{smaller block|Alas, you three, on me, ''threefold'' distress'd,
Pour all your tears! {{right|''Shak.,'' Rich. III., ii. 2. 86.}}}}

'''Thick and threefold.''' See ''thick.''

'''three-foot''' (thrē′fu̇t), ''a.'' [< ME. *''threfote,'' < AS. ''thriefēt, thrȳfēt, thrȳfēte,'' three-foot; as ''three'' + ''foot.'' Cf. ''tripod.''] 1. Measuring three feet: as, a ''three-foot'' rule.—2. Having three feet; three-footed.

{{smaller block|When on my ''three-foot'' stool I sit.
{{right|''Shak.,'' Cymbeline, iii. 3. 89.}}}}

'''three-footed''' (thrē′fu̇t″ed), ''a.'' [< ME. *''threfoted,'' < AS. ''thrȳfōtad,'' three-footed; as ''three'' + ''foot''

readings.c1s2:
—2. A symbol representing three units, as 3, III, or iii.—3. A playing-card bearing three spots or pips.—'''Inverse rule of three.''' {{smaller|See ''inverse.''}}—'''Rule of three.''' {{smaller|See ''rule''<sup>1</sup>.}}

'''three-aged''' (thrē′ājd), ''a.'' Living during three generations. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|Great Atreus' sons, Tydides fixt above,
With ''three-aged'' Nestor.
{{right|''Creech'', tr. of Manilius.}}}}

'''three-awned''' (thrē′ând), ''a.'' Having three awns.—'''Three-awned grass,''' {{smaller|an American grass, ''Aristida purpurascens;'' also, ''A. purpurea,'' purple three-awned grass. The latter is of some consequence as wild feed in the West. Also ''beard-grass.''}}

'''three-bearded''' (thrē′bēr″ded), ''a.'' Having three barbels: as, the ''three-bearded'' rockling, cod, or gade (a fish, ''Motella vulgaris'').

'''three-birds''' (thrē′bėrds), ''n.'' A species of toad-flax, ''Linaria triornithophora'' (see ''toad-flax''); also, ''Pogonia pendula.'' See ''Pogonia.''

'''three-bodied''' (thrē′bod″id), ''a.'' Having three bodies. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|I Caia Manlia, daughter to Caius Manlius, doe carie with me mine owne present, for I giue my condemned soule and life to the infernall ''three-bodyed'' Pluto.
{{right|''Guevara'', Letters (tr. by Hellowes, 1577), p. 336.}}}}

'''three-coat''' (thrē′kōt), ''a.'' Having or requiring three coats. {{smaller|(''a'') In ''plastering'', noting work which consists of pricking-up or roughing-in, floating, and a finishing coat. (''b'') In ''house-painting'', noting work when three successive layers of paint are required.}}

'''three-cornered''' (thrē′kôr″nėrd), ''a.'' 1. Having three corners or angles: as, a ''three-cornered'' hat.—2. In ''bot.'', triquetrous.—'''Three-cornered constituency,''' {{smaller|a constituency in which, while three members are returned at one election, each elector can vote for only two candidates. This enables a large minority to elect one of the three members, the majority electing}}

+ -''ed''<sup>2</sup>.] Having three feet: as, a ''three-footed'' stool.

'''three-girred''' (thrē′gėrd), ''a.'' Surrounded with three hoops. ''Burns.'' [Scotch.]

'''three-halfpence''' (thrē′hā′pe̤ns), ''n.'' An English silver coin of the value of three halfpence (3 cents), issued by Queen Elizabeth; also, a silver coin of William IV. and Queen Victoria, formerly issued for circulation in Ceylon.

'''three-handed''' (thrē′han″ded), ''a.'' 1. Having three hands.—2. Done, played, etc., with three hands or by three persons: as, ''three-handed'' euchre.—'''Three-handed boring.''' {{smaller|See ''boring.''}}

'''threehead†''', ''n.'' [ME. ''threhed'' (= G. ''dreiheit''); < ''three'' + ''head.''] Trinity.

{{smaller block|A God and ane Lord yn ''threhed,''
And thre persons yn anehede.
{{right|''Religious Pieces'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 59.}}}}

'''three-hooped''' (thrē′höpt), ''a.'' Having three hoops.—'''Three-hooped pot,''' {{smaller|a quart pot. See ''hoop''<sup>1</sup>, 5.}}

{{smaller block|The ''three-hooped pot'' shall have ten hoops; and I will make it felony to drink small beer.
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. VI., iv. 2. 72.}}}}

'''three-leaved''' (thrē′lēvd), ''a.'' In ''bot.'', having three leaves or leaflets, as many species of ''Trifolium;'' trifoliate or trifoliolate.—'''Three-leaved grass,''' {{smaller|an old book-name for clover.}}—'''Three-leaved ivy.''' {{smaller|See ''poison-ivy.''}}—'''Three-leaved nightshade,''' {{smaller|a plant of the genus ''Trillium.''}}

'''three-light''' (thrē′līt), ''n.'' A chandelier or candelabrum with three lamps for candles.

'''threeling''' (thrē′ling), ''n.'' Same as ''trilling'', 2.

'''three-lobed''' (thrē′lōbd), ''a.'' In ''bot.'', ''zoöl.'', and ''anat.'', having three lobes; trilobate.—'''Three-lobed malope.''' {{smaller|See ''Malope.''}}

